>[]($block.attrs("author-note"))
> Contrary to my [rambling review of Clair Obscur](rr-clair-obscur), there will be minimal
> spoilers from here on out. I will be talking about characters that you only meet a handful
> of hours into the game, but as they're such core parts of the narrative (and also feature
> prominently in the trailers), there won't be any major spoilers.

I feel like the most important things to know about Indiana Jones and the Great Circle
are, in order:
1. It is, very happily, much more *Raiders*/*Last Crusade* than *Crystal Skull*/*Dial
of Destiny* (to a point, though we'll talk about that in a bit).
2. Troy Baker's Indy is so uncannily spot on that I legitimately could have been fooled
into beliving that it was old recordings of Harrison Ford.
3. It delivers far more on the fantasy of being Indy than it does on the action movie tomb
raiding fantasy, where the Uncharted games are still far and away the king.

I really have to laud the game for delivering on all 3 of those things. At the risk of
leaning too far into the "it makes you FEEL like Batman" joke, it really does let you step
into Indy's leather jacket and fedora in a pretty impressive way. All the whip &
wise-cracking that you'd expect is there. The fisticuffs, while they stale quickly, are
done with such care for the audio in particular that it puts you right into one of the
movies. And the slightly slapstick animations when you crack a fascist over the head from
behind with a mop never get old (largely because whacking fascists in the balls and/or
face will always be a productive use of time, both in a game and the real world). I wasn't
really sure about the first person perspective of the game going into it, but within an
hour I was solidly convinced that it was the only way they could have made it as true to
the movies as they did.

The gameplay itself can only be described as "competent." The combat works and is
entertaining enough, though it stales quickly. The puzzles are very hit or miss: none of
them are particularly difficult, but it's about half and half between clever ideas that
make you at least think for a few seconds and "find the magic place to press e," which can
be a bit frustrating. The stealth elements in particular are a low point for me as well.
In areas where the level is designed around plenty of spots to dissapear in the crowd or
through a nearby doorway it isn't too bad (Vatican City is particularly good at this). But
as you move through the game the inconsistency of enemies either being able to zero in on
your location like a guided missle or watch you bludgeon their friend over the head 2 feet
in front of them without batting an eye starts to be a bit grating, enough so that by the
end my strategy was largely to sprint past large groups and then hide under a table until
they lost interest.

As for the story, I really want to do my best to avoid spoiling any of the twists and
turns because The Great Circle really hits the nail on the head in being a prime Indy
story. That said, the main companion character, Gina, is excellent (so long as it's during
cutscenes/scripted sequences and not in the open level exploration, where she can be
incredibly annoying). She also is *competent*, which is a really nice change of pace. It's
not just Indy explaining everything to her over and over, she actively contributes a ton
to the story. Voss, the Nazi villain of this Indy story, is also a high point. He
perfectly plays the
sorta-cringe-kid-who-was-really-into-anime-but-somehow-got-a-high-ranking-government-job,
with appropriate levels of both menace and intelligence that don't make him feel like too
much of a caricature.

I do think the "final find" that the game builds up too leans perhaps a smidge too much on
some fantastical elements. Obviously the Indiana Jones franchise is no stranger to
injecting some magic into its climaxes, but in Raiders and Last Crusade both it at least
felt somewhat carefully understated, at least as opposed to Crystal Skull and Dial of
Destiny (I am intentionally not mentioning Temple of Doom because I find it forgettable).
The Great Circle definitely sets itself on the path of Raiders/Crusade, but ends up
leaning a bit in the other direction. In the grand scheme of things though, it's at least
not egregious, and disbelief can still be easily suspended.

Pound for pound, I still think that the Uncharted games are unbeatable in delivering on
the "globetrotting treasure hunter" fantasy, and Uncharted 4 in particular has such an
excellent narrative and characters that I don't think Great Circle stood much of a chance.
But that doesn't mean that Great Circle is a subpar game: on the contrary I think it is
exactly what it was supposed to be. Competent, fun, delivers on the Indiana Jones fantasy
to a T, and paced exactly well enough to feel like one of the movies on steroids. I
definitely recommend it, though I think it's one that I would go for on sale rather than
full price.
